---
title: Watch a Video
category: activity
status: draft
order: 1
icon: video
summary: Effective educational videos combine sound instructional design with user-centred media practices and system architecture.
---

{% pattern-list role="checklist" layer="content" %}
# Content design

## The video follows a logical flow that supports learning objectives

Organise the video around explicit intended learning outcomes. Each section, explanation, example and visual should contribute to what learners are expected to know or perform. Remove content that is interesting but unnecessary. This applies the coherence principle: learners generally perform better when extraneous information is excluded.[^mayer]

Use a structure appropriate to the subject rather than enforcing a rigid template. A useful default may follow [BOPPPS][boppps-model] or [CARD][card-model] model and consist of a brief orientation, clearly identified sections, explicit transitions and a final synthesis or application prompt. 

Break complex explanations into meaningful segments and allow learners to process one segment before moving to the next. A meta-analysis found small-to-moderate positive effects of learner-paced segmentation on retention and transfer.[^segmenting] Guidance for educational-video production similarly recommends keeping videos focused on their learning goals and using signals to highlight important concepts.[^video-guidelines]

[^mayer]: [Principles for reducing extraneous processing](https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108894333.019 "Fiorella, L., & Mayer, R. E. (2021). Principles for reducing extraneous processing in multimedia learning: Coherence, signaling, redundancy, spatial contiguity, and temporal contiguity principles. In R. E. Mayer & L. Fiorella (Eds.), The Cambridge handbook of multimedia learning (3rd ed., pp. 185–198). Cambridge University Press. This chapter supports the coherence-principle claim that excluding irrelevant material reduces extraneous processing.")

[^segmenting]: [Meta-analysis of the segmenting effect](https://doi.org/10.1007/s10648-018-9456-4 "Rey, G. D., Beege, M., Nebel, S., Wirzberger, M., Schmitt, T. H., & Schneider, S. (2019). A meta-analysis of the segmenting effect. Educational Psychology Review, 31(2), 389–419. This meta-analysis supports the cited positive retention and transfer effects of learner-paced segmentation.")

[^video-guidelines]: [Effective educational videos](https://doi.org/10.1187/cbe.16-03-0125 "Brame, C. J. (2016). Effective educational videos: Principles and guidelines for maximizing student learning from video content. CBE—Life Sciences Education, 15(4), es6. This review supports focused learning goals and signaling important concepts in educational video.")


## The duration of the video is concise and reasonable (6 min sweetspot)

Shorter videos boost engagement and learning. A large-scale MOOC study found median viewing times ≈6 minutes, regardless of longer actual length[^mooc], and another study showed students watching short (~8 min) videos scored 9% higher than those watching ~55 min lectures.

Brief videos match attention spans better (studies indicate drop-offs after 6-9 min) and encourage active re-engagement. Quality Matters and other experts recommend keeping lessons under ~6-10 min. Short clips can be easily rewatched, supplemented with activities and reused in other learniung paths.

[^mooc]: [An empirical study of MOOC videos](https://doi.org/10.1145/2556325.2566239 "Guo, P. J., Kim, J., & Rubin, R. (2014). How video production affects student engagement: An empirical study of MOOC videos. Proceedings of the First ACM Conference on Learning @ Scale, 41–50. This study supports the approximately six-minute median-engagement finding.")

## Branded intro/outro is not longer than 3 seconds

Keep any intros/outros extremely short. Use minimal animations or a logo flash of 1-2 seconds if needed. Very short intros avoid losing attention. Viewers generally "want to jump right into content". 

Minimising non-instructional sequences eliminates unnecessary delays and prevents early drop-off. Quick branding (e.g. a flash of a logo) can provide professionalism without boredom. Especially when your course contain 30+ videos with exactly the same intro & outro in each.

A brilliant example of the extremely short but still speaking out intro in instructional video is the Google Career Certification courses on Coursera.[^google-courses]

[^google-courses]: [Google Career Certification course example](https://www.coursera.org/learn/foundations-data "Google Career Certificates. (n.d.). Foundations: Data, Data, Everywhere. Coursera. This course is an example of extremely short intro/outro elements.")

## A brief targeted text introduction accompanies the video

Briefly explain the video’s purpose, relevance, intended audience and any required prior knowledge. Use language and examples appropriate to the learners, and avoid repeating the video’s opening narration.[^pretraining]

 When the same video is reused across different courses or activities, tailor the introduction to place it in the current learning context and emphasise its specific value for that use.

[^pretraining]: [The pre-training principle](https://doi.org/10.1017/cbo9781139547369.016 "Mayer, R. E., & Pilegard, C. (2014). Principles for managing essential processing in multimedia learning: Segmenting, pre-training, and modality principles. In The Cambridge handbook of multimedia learning (pp. 316–344). Cambridge University Press. This chapter supports pre-training learners in relevant component knowledge before multimedia instruction.")


## Color contrast is checked and accessible for texts and visuals

Ensure all on-screen text and visuals have high contrast and are easily distinguishable. For example, use dark text on a light background with a contrast ratio at least 4.5:1. 

Sufficient contrast makes text and graphics readable for low-vision and colour-blind learners, and under various lighting. It is an [accessibility][accessibility] requirement to accommodate ~8% of men (and 0.5% of women) with colour-vision deficiency. High contrast also reduces eye strain for all users.[^wcag] 

[^wcag]: [Web Content Accessibility Guidelines](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG21/ "World Wide Web Consortium (W3C). (2025). Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.1. W3C Recommendation. This standard supports the 4.5:1 minimum contrast requirement for normal text.")

## Audio is clear and free from distracting noise

Ensure speech and essential sounds are intelligible, with consistent volume and no clipping, excessive reverberation or background noise that masks the content. Use music and contextual sounds only when they serve a clear instructional purpose.[^audio-quality]

[^audio-quality]: [How audio quality influences perceptions](https://doi.org/10.1177/1075547018759345 "Newman, E. J., & Schwarz, N. (2018). Good sound, good research: How audio quality influences perceptions of the research and researcher. Science Communication, 40(2), 246–257. This experiment shows degraded audio worsens perceived research and researcher quality.")


## Video quality is stable and visual dynamics support learning

Ensure that all learning-relevant details remain clear and legible, without distracting camera movement, blur, lighting changes or compression artefacts. Use changes in shot, pace, animation and visual focus to demonstrate processes, signal structure or direct attention—not merely to make the video appear more dynamic.

Avoid both prolonged static presentation and excessive editing. The appropriate pace and amount of movement depend on the subject, learners and complexity of the material; production polish should never compete with the explanation or introduce unnecessary cognitive load.


## Accurate closed captions or subtitles are available

Provide synchronized, selectable closed captions for all speech and meaningful sounds. Review automatically generated captions for accuracy, timing, speaker identification, terminology, numbers and non-speech information. Provide translated subtitles as separate language tracks where needed.[^captions][^caption-benefits]

Where useful, add an interactive transcript that learners can search and use to navigate to specific points in the video. A transcript complements but does not replace synchronized captions.

[^captions]: [Captions and subtitles](https://www.w3.org/WAI/media/av/captions/ "World Wide Web Consortium (W3C). (2024). Captions/subtitles. Web Accessibility Initiative. This guidance supports synchronized captions for speech and meaningful sound.")

[^caption-benefits]: [Video captions benefit everyone](https://doi.org/10.1177/2372732215602130 "Gernsbacher, M. A. (2015). Video captions benefit everyone. Policy Insights from the Behavioral and Brain Sciences, 2(1), 195–202. This review supports the broad learning and accessibility benefits of video captions.")


## Essential visual content is explicitly described in the narration

Verbally identify and describe visual information needed to understand the video, including actions, diagrams, data, interface changes and relevant on-screen text. Use precise references instead of phrases such as “as you can see” or “over here,” so the explanation remains understandable without relying entirely on sight.[^visual-description]

Describe only information that contributes to meaning. Where essential visual content cannot be incorporated naturally into the narration, provide a separate audio description or descriptive transcript.

[^visual-description]: [Description of visual information](https://www.w3.org/WAI/media/av/description/ "World Wide Web Consortium (W3C). (2024). Description of visual information. Web Accessibility Initiative. This guidance supports describing essential visual information for people who cannot see it.")


## Key outcomes and takeaways accompany the video

Provide a concise list of what the video covers and what learners should understand or be able to do. Keep it visible alongside the video so learners can assess its relevance before watching and quickly revisit the essential points afterwards.[^video-summaries]

Include only the central outcomes and ideas. Do not use the summary as a substitute for watching when demonstrations, explanations or context are essential. Support materials should extend access, review or application—not simply duplicate the video. Transcripts improve accessibility and searchability, while active-learning materials can strengthen comprehension and transfer.

[^video-summaries]: [Writing and reading summaries of educational videos](https://doi.org/10.1145/3170358.3170382 "van der Zee, T., Davis, D., Saab, N., Giesbers, B., Ginn, J., van der Sluis, F., Paas, F., & Admiraal, W. (2018). Evaluating retrieval practice in a MOOC: How writing and reading summaries of videos affects student learning. Proceedings of the 8th International Conference on Learning Analytics and Knowledge, 216–225. This study examines post-viewing writing and reading of summaries.")


## Relevant support materials provided with the video

Provide materials that help learners access, review or apply the content, such as a searchable transcript, accessible copies of essential visuals, slides, references, key terminology, worksheets or practical examples.

Where appropriate, include a focused retrieval, reflection or application activity. Keep support materials concise, current and clearly connected to the video’s intended learning outcomes.[^active-learning]

[^active-learning]: [Active learning strategies in video learning](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.edurev.2025.100708 "Zhang, Y., Li, R., Pi, Z., & Yang, J. (2025). Active learning strategies in video learning: A meta-analysis. Educational Research Review, 48, 100708. This meta-analysis supports adding active learning activities to video-based learning.")


## Localized versions are available for target regions

Provide localized content where language or regional context could limit understanding or relevance. Depending on learners’ needs, this may include translated subtitles or transcripts, additional audio tracks, or fully localized video versions.[^multilingual-education][^captioned-video]

Adapt terminology, examples, units, cultural references and on-screen text where necessary—not only the spoken words. Human-review translations, label each language and locale clearly, and keep all localized versions synchronized when the source video changes.[^captions]

[^multilingual-education]: [Global guidance on multilingual education](https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/languages-matter-global-guidance-multilingual-education "UNESCO. (2025). Languages matter: Global guidance on multilingual education. UNESCO. This guidance supports reducing language barriers through multilingual education.")

[^captioned-video]: [Captioned video for second-language learning](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.system.2013.07.013 "Montero Perez, M., Van Den Noortgate, W., & Desmet, P. (2013). Captioned video for L2 listening and vocabulary learning: A meta-analysis. System, 41(3), 720–739. This meta-analysis supports captioned video as an aid to second-language listening and vocabulary learning.")

{% /pattern-list %}

{% pattern-list role="checklist" layer="interactions" %}
# Interactions design

## Video duration is shown before playback

Display the video’s total running time before the learner starts it, so they can assess its relevance and plan when to watch it. If pauses, exercises or reflection are expected, show the estimated total activity time separately.[^mooc]


## Longer videos include linked timestamps or chapters

Divide longer or multi-topic videos into meaningful sections with descriptive labels and links to their starting points. Chapters help learners understand the structure, locate relevant content and return to specific explanations without searching through the entire video.[^video-contents]

Align chapter boundaries with conceptual transitions rather than arbitrary time intervals. Ensure timestamps and chapter controls are accessible by keyboard and assistive technologies.

[^video-contents]: [Interactive tables of contents in educational videos](https://doi.org/10.1111/bjet.13164 "Cojean, S., & Jamet, E. (2022). Does an interactive table of contents promote learning from videos? A study of consultation strategies and learning outcomes. British Journal of Educational Technology, 53(2), 269–285. This study supports tables of contents and interactive navigation for recall and relevant reviewing behaviour.")

## Playback speed is easy to adjust

Allow learners to slow down or speed up the video using clearly labelled, keyboard-accessible controls. Provide the normal 1× rate and a practical range of slower and faster options while preserving the natural pitch and intelligibility of speech.[^media-accessibility]

Slower playback can help non-native speakers process unfamiliar vocabulary, accents and sentence structures. [^non-native-speed]

Do not force accelerated playback: faster speeds can save time but may reduce learning performance, particularly with dense or unfamiliar material, however the cost of it is not much and often non-significant on x1.25-x1.5.[^playback-speed]

[^non-native-speed]: [Accent and speech-rate effects in English as a lingua franca](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.system.2014.07.015 "Matsuura, H., Chiba, R., Mahoney, S., & Rilling, S. (2014). Accent and speech rate effects in English as a lingua franca. System, 46, 143–150. This study shows that speech rate and accent affect English-as-a-lingua-franca listening.")

[^playback-speed]: [Increasing video lecture playback speed can impair test performance](https://doi.org/10.1007/s10648-025-10003-9 "Tharumalingam, T., Roberts, B. R. T., Fawcett, J. M., & Risko, E. F. (2025). Increasing video lecture playback speed can impair test performance: A meta-analysis. Educational Psychology Review, 37(2), Article 35. This meta-analysis supports the warning that accelerated educational video can reduce test performance.")

## Video quality adapts to available bandwidth and can be adjusted

Use adaptive bitrate streaming to maintain stable playback as network conditions change, and allow learners to select the video quality manually when needed. Provide multiple quality levels suitable for different devices, connection speeds and data limits.[^adaptive-streaming][^stream-quality]

Ensure that essential text, diagrams, interface details and demonstrations remain legible at every supported quality level. Where low-resolution video cannot preserve necessary information, provide accessible supporting materials or a downloadable alternative.

[^adaptive-streaming]: [HTTP Live Streaming](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/http-live-streaming "Apple Inc. (n.d.). HTTP Live Streaming. Apple Developer Documentation. This documentation supports adaptive bitrate playback through multiple stream variants.")

[^stream-quality]: [Video stream quality impacts viewer behaviour](https://doi.org/10.1109/TNET.2013.2281542 "Krishnan, S. S., & Sitaraman, R. K. (2013). Video stream quality impacts viewer behavior: Inferring causality using quasi-experimental designs. IEEE/ACM Transactions on Networking, 21(6), 2001–2014. This study supports the importance of stream quality for viewer behaviour.")


## Seeking is available by mouse, touch and keyboard

Allow learners to move forwards and backwards through the video using mouse, touch and keyboard controls. Seeking should not depend only on dragging the timeline; provide accessible buttons or shortcuts, visible keyboard focus and a clearly communicated playback position.[^media-player][^keyboard-access]

Ensure controls have sufficiently large touch targets and can be identified and operated by assistive technologies.[^target-size]

[^media-player]: [Accessible media players](https://www.w3.org/WAI/media/av/player/ "World Wide Web Consortium (W3C). (2024). Media players. Web Accessibility Initiative. This guidance supports accessible media controls and player operation.")

[^keyboard-access]: [Understanding keyboard accessibility](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/keyboard.html "World Wide Web Consortium (W3C). (2026). Understanding Success Criterion 2.1.1: Keyboard. Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2. This guidance supports keyboard operability of media controls.")

[^target-size]: [Understanding target size](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/target-size-minimum.html "World Wide Web Consortium (W3C). (2026). Understanding Success Criterion 2.5.8: Target Size (Minimum). Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2. This guidance supports sufficiently large touch targets.")

For video, the progress indicator should double as an accessible seek control: current position and duration should be understandable visually and to assistive technologies. ([W3C][1])

## Watching progress is clearly visualised

Show the learner’s current position and overall progress throughout playback using a visible timeline or progress indicator, with elapsed and total time available where appropriate. Make the current position understandable without relying on colour alone.[^seek-progress]

When the progress indicator also supports seeking, make it operable by mouse, touch and keyboard and expose the current position in a meaningful time format to assistive technologies.[^accessible-seek]

[^seek-progress]: [The impact of progress indicators on task completion](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.intcom.2010.03.001 "Conrad, F. G., Couper, M. P., Tourangeau, R., & Peytchev, A. (2010). The Impact of Progress Indicators on Task Completion. Interacting with Computers, 22(5), 417–427.")

[^accessible-seek]: [Media seek slider example](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/slider/examples/slider-seek/ "World Wide Web Consortium. ARIA Authoring Practices Guide: Media Seek Slider Example. Guidance on communicating current media position and enabling keyboard-accessible seeking.")

[1]: https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/slider/examples/slider-seek/?utm_source=chatgpt.com "Media Seek Slider Example | APG | WAI | W3C"


## Video is large enough for all on-screen content to remain readable

Set the default player size so learners can clearly read instructional text, captions, diagrams, interface elements and controls without entering full-screen mode. The video may extend beyond the surrounding text column when its visual content requires more space.[^video-content]

Keep the player responsive, preserve its aspect ratio and avoid horizontal page scrolling on smaller screens. Test readability across supported viewport sizes and provide full-screen viewing or accessible alternatives for complex visuals.[^reflow][^media-accessibility]

[^video-content]: [Audio and video content accessibility](https://www.w3.org/WAI/media/av/av-content/ "World Wide Web Consortium (W3C). (2024). Audio and video content. Web Accessibility Initiative. This guidance supports making audio and video content accessible through appropriate alternatives.")

[^reflow]: [Understanding reflow](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/reflow.html "World Wide Web Consortium (W3C). (2026). Understanding Success Criterion 1.4.10: Reflow. Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2. This guidance supports responsive layouts without unnecessary two-dimensional scrolling.")

## Full-screen playback is available

Allow learners to enlarge the video to full screen when they need to inspect text, diagrams, interfaces or demonstrations. Captions, audio options and playback controls must remain available and accessible by keyboard and assistive technologies in full-screen mode.[^media-accessibility][^media-player]

[^media-accessibility]: [Media accessibility user requirements](https://www.w3.org/TR/media-accessibility-reqs/ "World Wide Web Consortium (W3C) Protocols and Formats Working Group. (2015). Media Accessibility User Requirements. W3C Working Group Note. This note supports keyboard-operable controls, time-scale modification, captions, descriptions, and scalable accessible media.")


## The video transcript can be searched and used for navigation

Provide a searchable, synchronised transcript that lets learners find words, phrases or topics and jump directly to the corresponding point in the video. Highlight the current transcript passage during playback and allow selecting text or timestamps to seek within the video.[^interactive-transcript]

Keep transcript navigation operable by keyboard and assistive technologies, and use headings or other meaningful structure for longer transcripts so learners can quickly locate relevant sections.[^transcript-navigation]

[^interactive-transcript]: [Interactive transcripts in accessible media players](https://www.w3.org/WAI/media/av/player/ "World Wide Web Consortium. Media Players. Web Accessibility Initiative. Interactive transcripts can highlight text as it is spoken and allow users to select transcript text to navigate directly to the corresponding point in the video.")

[^transcript-navigation]: [Making transcripts more useful](https://www.w3.org/WAI/media/av/transcripts/ "World Wide Web Consortium. Transcripts. Web Accessibility Initiative. Guidance recommends adding headings, links and timestamps where they improve navigation and usability.")


## All video interactions are tested on mobile and touch screens

Test playback, seeking, captions, chapters, speed, quality and full-screen controls on supported mobile devices in both portrait and landscape orientations. Ensure controls remain visible, responsive and operable by touch without requiring precise gestures.[^mobile-accessibility]

Provide sufficiently large touch targets, prevent controls from obscuring essential content and verify equivalent functionality across current iOS and Android browsers.[^target-size]

[^mobile-accessibility]: [Mobile accessibility guidance](https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/mobile/ "World Wide Web Consortium (W3C). (2025). Mobile accessibility: How WCAG 2.0 and other W3C/WAI guidelines apply to mobile. Web Accessibility Initiative. This guidance supports applying accessibility requirements to mobile web content.")

## Playback can resume from where the learner stopped

Save the learner’s latest playback position and offer to resume from it when they return, while preserving the option to restart the video. This supports educational videos that learners watch across multiple sessions.[^multiple-sessions]

For longer videos, consider resuming slightly before the saved position or at the beginning of the current chapter to restore context. Keep saved progress synchronized across devices where learners are signed in, and reset or migrate it when the video is substantially revised.

[^multiple-sessions]: [Educational video engagement across multiple sessions](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105148 "Parker, M. J., Bunch, M., & Pike, A. (2024). Is anybody watching: A multi-factor motivational framework for educational video engagement. Computers & Education, 222, 105148. This article examines factors associated with educational-video engagement.")

## Playback preferences are saved and applied across videos

Preserve the learner’s selected captions, audio or subtitle language, playback speed, volume or mute state, and autoplay preference across videos and sessions where the browser and device permit it. For signed-in learners, synchronise relevant preferences across devices when appropriate.[^media-accessibility]

Apply only settings supported by the next video, provide an easy way to change or reset them, and keep autoplay opt-in rather than enabling it by default.[^autoplay]

[^autoplay]: [HTML media autoplay guidance](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/HTMLMediaElement/autoplay "MDN contributors. (2025). HTMLMediaElement: autoplay property. MDN Web Docs. This documentation explains autoplay behavior and browser policies that may block audible autoplay.")

## The paused state clearly supports continued viewing

When playback is paused, preserve the current frame and position, show accurate progress, and make a clear, accessible **Continue watching** action prominent. Where useful, identify the current chapter or next section so learners can quickly recover context and resume.[^task-resumption]

Use neutral encouragement rather than autoplay, countdowns, guilt-based messages or misleading progress cues. Progress feedback can support continuation when it matches learners’ expectations, but may increase abandonment when progress appears slower than expected.[^progress-feedback]

[^task-resumption]: [Interruption, recall and resumption](https://doi.org/10.1057/s41599-025-05000-w "Ghibellini, R., & Meier, B. (2025). Interruption, recall and resumption: A meta-analysis of the Zeigarnik and Ovsiankina effects. Humanities and Social Sciences Communications, 12(1), Article 962. This meta-analysis finds a general tendency to resume interrupted tasks.")

[^progress-feedback]: [The impact of progress indicators on task completion](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.intcom.2010.03.001 "Conrad, F. G., Couper, M. P., Tourangeau, R., & Peytchev, A. (2010). The impact of progress indicators on task completion. Interacting with Computers, 22(5), 417–427. This experiment supports the claim that progress-indicator design can affect task completion.")

## Watched videos are clearly distinguished

Mark watched videos with a persistent visual status, such as a **Watched** label, checkmark or progress indicator. Keep the video title and thumbnail recognisable, and do not use colour as the only way to communicate the status.[^use-of-colour]

Expose the watched state programmatically so it can be identified by screen readers and other assistive technologies.[^status-messages]

[^use-of-colour]: [Understanding use of colour](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/use-of-color "World Wide Web Consortium (W3C). (2025). Understanding Success Criterion 1.4.1: Use of color. Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2. This guidance supports not using colour as the sole means of conveying watched status.")

[^status-messages]: [Understanding status messages](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/status-messages "World Wide Web Consortium (W3C). (2026). Understanding Success Criterion 4.1.3: Status messages. Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2. This guidance supports exposing dynamic status information to assistive technologies.")

## The next learning activity is clearly emphasised after video completion

After the video ends, present a prominent next action, such as watching the next video, reviewing key takeaways, opening support materials or completing a retrieval, reflection or application activity.[^active-learning]

Keep the action accessible and relevant to the learning sequence. Do not rely on autoplay or automatically move learners away from the completed video before they can review it.[^focus-order]

[^focus-order]: [Understanding focus order](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/focus-order.html "World Wide Web Consortium (W3C). (2026). Understanding Success Criterion 2.4.3: Focus order. Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2. This guidance supports maintaining a meaningful keyboard focus order.")

{% /pattern-list %}

{% pattern-list role="checklist" layer="system" %}
# System design

## The video is protected from unauthorised access

Restrict access according to the intended audience using authentication and role-based permissions. Verify authorisation for every protected request, including video streams, files, captions and downloads, and prevent unintended public indexing or direct-link access.[^access-control]

Configure equivalent restrictions in any external video-hosting service used by the platform. For example, Vimeo may require viewing and domain-level embed privacy settings, while YouTube visibility settings have different limitations: an unlisted video can be accessed by anyone with its link, whereas private videos require explicitly authorised Google accounts.[^vimeo-privacy][^youtube-privacy]

[^access-control]: [Authorization guidance](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html "OWASP Foundation. (n.d.). Authorization Cheat Sheet. OWASP Cheat Sheet Series. This cheat sheet supports request-by-request authorization and least-privilege access controls.")

[^vimeo-privacy]: [Vimeo video privacy settings](https://help.vimeo.com/hc/en-us/articles/12426199699985-About-video-privacy-settings "Vimeo. (2026). About video privacy settings. Vimeo Help Center. This article supports Vimeo visibility and domain-level embed privacy settings.")

[^youtube-privacy]: [YouTube video privacy settings](https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/157177?hl=en "YouTube Help. (n.d.). Change video privacy settings. Google. This article explains that unlisted links are shareable and private videos are limited to invited accounts.")

## Video is reliably accessible in all target regions

Deliver the video through a CDN (contnet delivery netwoek) or hosting service that provides stable playback in every intended region. Test availability, start-up time, buffering, video quality, captions and audio tracks from representative locations and network conditions.[^cdn-delivery]

Verify regional availability separately for external platforms such as YouTube or Vimeo, as videos or platform services may be restricted or unavailable in particular countries. Where reliable access cannot be guaranteed, provide an alternative host or accessible fallback.[^youtube-regions][^vimeo-regions]

[^cdn-delivery]: [How CloudFront delivers content](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/HowCloudFrontWorks.html "Amazon Web Services, Inc. (n.d.). How CloudFront delivers content. Amazon CloudFront Developer Guide. This guide explains CDN delivery through edge locations.")

[^youtube-regions]: [Video is not available in my country or region](https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/92571?hl=en "YouTube Help. (n.d.). Video isn't available in my country or region. Google. This article confirms that YouTube availability can vary by country or region.")

[^vimeo-regions]: [Vimeo availability and regional restrictions](https://help.vimeo.com/hc/en-us/articles/18332106845329-Vimeo-availability-and-regional-restrictions "Vimeo. (2026). Vimeo availability and regional restrictions. Vimeo Help Center. This article documents regional availability and restrictions.")

## Post-playback recommendations are disabled or controlled

Configure embedded players so the video ends without unrelated recommendations, advertising or links that distract learners from the activity. Where supported, use an empty end screen or limit suggested content to an approved channel, playlist or collection.[^vimeo-end-screens]

Check the current limitations of each public platform. YouTube does not allow related videos to be disabled completely: `rel=0` only limits recommendations to videos from the same channel. Use another hosting solution when complete control of the post-playback experience is required.[^youtube-recommendations]

[^vimeo-end-screens]: [Vimeo end-screen settings](https://help.vimeo.com/hc/en-us/articles/12426231346449-About-end-screens "Vimeo. (2025). About end screens. Vimeo Help Center. This article supports configurable end screens, including an Empty option.")

[^youtube-recommendations]: [YouTube embedded-player parameters](https://developers.google.com/youtube/player_parameters#rel "Google for Developers. (n.d.). YouTube Embedded Players and Player Parameters. Google. This reference states that rel=0 limits related videos to the same channel rather than disabling them.")

## Video completion and viewing analytics are available

Track a clearly defined completion event for each learner and provide aggregated analytics for the target audience. Where useful, report starts, viewing progress, completions, repeated sections, drop-off points and playback errors.[^learning-analytics]

Treat completion as evidence of viewing behaviour — not attention, understanding or mastery. Collect identifiable analytics only for defined purposes, restrict access and retention, and use aggregated or pseudonymised data whenever individual records are unnecessary.[^analytics-limits][^data-protection]

[^learning-analytics]: [Caliper Analytics](https://www.1edtech.org/standards/caliper "1EdTech Consortium. (n.d.). Caliper Analytics®. 1EdTech. This standard describes learning activity and product-usage events, including media activity.")

[^analytics-limits]: [Making sense of video analytics](https://doi.org/10.19173/irrodl.v16i1.1976 "Giannakos, M. N., Chorianopoulos, K., & Chrisochoides, N. (2015). Making sense of video analytics: Lessons learned from clickstream interactions, attitudes, and learning outcome in a video-assisted course. The International Review of Research in Open and Distributed Learning, 16(1). This study relates video clickstream data to attitudes and learning outcomes.")

[^data-protection]: [Principles of the GDPR](https://commission.europa.eu/law/law-topic/data-protection/information-business-and-organisations/principles-gdpr_en "European Commission. (n.d.). Principles of the GDPR. European Commission. This guidance supports purpose limitation, data minimisation, storage limitation, and security controls.")

## Native mobile playback is tested and preserves intended interactions

Test video playback with native iOS and Android players, including inline and full-screen modes. Verify that entering, leaving or switching playback modes does not lose progress, dismiss unsaved work, reload the page or disrupt surrounding activities, instructions and navigation.[^ios-playback][^android-playback]

Ensure that captions, audio tracks, playback controls and accessibility features remain available. When native playback temporarily hides surrounding content, learners must be able to return to the same content state and continue the activity.[^media-accessibility]

[^ios-playback]: [Delivering video content for Safari](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/webkit/delivering-video-content-for-safari "Apple Inc. (n.d.). Delivering video content for Safari. Apple Developer Documentation. This documentation supports considering Safari inline and full-screen playback controls.")

[^android-playback]: [Create a media player using Media3 ExoPlayer](https://developer.android.com/media/implement/playback-app "Android Developers. (2026). Create a basic media player app using Media3 ExoPlayer. Android Developers. This documentation supports building Android media playback.")

## Content language is correctly identified in HTML

Set the primary language of each page with a valid BCP 47 `lang` attribute on the root `<html>` element. This enables screen readers and other assistive technologies to apply the correct pronunciation and language rules.[^page-language]

When a passage or interface element uses a different language, set `lang` on the nearest containing element. Do not mark proper names, technical terms or commonly adopted foreign words unless a language change is needed for correct interpretation.[^language-parts]

[^page-language]: [Using the language attribute on the HTML element](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Techniques/html/H57.html "World Wide Web Consortium (W3C). (2026). Technique H57: Using the lang attribute on the html element. Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2. This technique supports setting the page’s primary language on the html element.")

[^language-parts]: [Using language attributes to identify language changes](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Techniques/html/H58 "World Wide Web Consortium (W3C). (2025). Technique H58: Using language attributes to identify changes in the human language. Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2. This technique supports marking language changes on the nearest containing element when needed.")

